WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:06.760
Welcome to The Debate. Today, we are diving into the intellectual journey mapped out in

00:06.760 --> 00:12.480
Seung Hee Yoon's essay, Coming Back to the Human. But before we, you know, get into the heavy

00:12.480 --> 00:18.360
philosophy, I want you to imagine taking a sledgehammer to your own home. Sounds messy.

00:18.680 --> 00:24.020
Very. You strike the walls, tearing down the drywall of your culture. You smash the support

00:24.020 --> 00:30.340
beams of your evolutionary biology, and you absolutely shatter the concrete foundation of

00:30.340 --> 00:37.160
cosmic meaning. You are utterly ruthless in this demolition. And you don't stop until you are left

00:37.160 --> 00:43.540
standing in an empty dirt lot. The Void. Which is, I mean, a terrifying place to be.

00:44.040 --> 00:50.040
You've essentially used pure lyic to prove that absolutely nothing matters. Exactly. But then,

00:50.040 --> 00:55.720
after surveying this total emptiness, you do something strange. You pick up a trowel,

00:56.080 --> 01:01.340
and you decide to rebuild the exact same house, brick by brick, in the exact same spot.

01:01.680 --> 01:02.020
Right.

01:02.940 --> 01:10.700
So, the central question we are exploring today is this. Is that decision to rebuild an act of

01:10.700 --> 01:15.920
ultimate, undounded freedom? Or did you just get a little too cold standing out in the void?

01:15.920 --> 01:22.040
Yeah. Are you freely choosing the structure, or are you just retreating to the only shelter you've

01:22.040 --> 01:22.480
ever known?

01:22.960 --> 01:29.460
Right. Well, I'll be defending the essay's framework today. I believe this return to human values

01:29.460 --> 01:35.380
represents a profound exercise of unbounded agency. It's a demonstration of practical wisdom

01:35.380 --> 01:38.840
that completely transcends that initial rational demolition.

01:38.840 --> 01:44.460
And I take the opposing view. Because while the author's initial demolition of our inherited values

01:44.460 --> 01:49.780
is just breathtakingly precise, I argue that this so-called return lacks the rigorous,

01:50.140 --> 01:53.320
logical foundation that's demanded of everything else in the text.

01:53.660 --> 01:54.820
You think it's a cop-out?

01:55.020 --> 02:01.020
I think it risks being a sophisticated disguise, yeah? It looks like a surrender to the very

02:01.020 --> 02:05.720
biological and cultural conditioning the author claims to have successfully transcended.

02:05.720 --> 02:10.440
Okay. Well, to really unpack why I think the author succeeds here, we have to look at the

02:10.440 --> 02:15.860
mechanics of the journey. The author constructs this two-part mechanism for living authentically.

02:16.600 --> 02:21.720
First, rationality acts as a sort of universal solvent. It dissolves our cosmic illusions.

02:22.020 --> 02:23.380
Which is the sledgehammer part.

02:23.500 --> 02:29.700
Exactly. If you think about the things we value, romantic love, ambition, patriotism, we tend to think

02:29.700 --> 02:34.620
they have some inherent universal importance. We look at a couple dedicating their lives to each other

02:34.620 --> 02:36.220
and call it a cosmic destiny.

02:36.500 --> 02:41.780
Right. But the text rightly points out that if you apply strict logic to that, the romance evaporates.

02:42.200 --> 02:47.380
It reveals it as an evolved trait. I mean, it's just DNA sequences optimizing for procreation.

02:47.820 --> 02:49.140
It's blind biology.

02:49.660 --> 02:55.200
Precisely. And pure reason leaves you with that void. Reason dissolves. It does not reconstruct.

02:55.700 --> 02:58.300
And this is where the brilliance of the text really comes in.

02:58.300 --> 03:02.400
It recognizes that you cannot run a human life on just one engine.

03:02.900 --> 03:03.220
Mm-hmm.

03:03.800 --> 03:07.460
The first engine, reason, gets you out of the atmosphere of illusion.

03:07.820 --> 03:12.440
But you need a second engine to actually navigate the space you find yourself in.

03:12.580 --> 03:15.000
The author identifies the second engine as wisdom.

03:15.280 --> 03:19.800
We might call it Aristotelian practical wisdom or Mahayana Buddhist compassion.

03:20.260 --> 03:24.060
So returning to the human marketplace, going back to your job, loving your family,

03:24.060 --> 03:28.160
it isn't a failure of logic. It is a deliberate, eyes-wide-open choice.

03:28.740 --> 03:33.680
I come at it from a different way. Because while that two-engine metaphor sounds really elegant,

03:34.360 --> 03:37.800
I find the return in this essay to be philosophically unstable.

03:38.380 --> 03:39.040
Unstable how?

03:39.340 --> 03:43.340
Well, let me be clear. I am completely on board with the initial disillusion.

03:43.920 --> 03:48.660
The text is unflinching. If you look at your daily grind, your desire to be a great entrepreneur,

03:49.000 --> 03:53.640
or even your deep emotional bonds, these are just configurations of the nervous system.

03:53.640 --> 03:55.360
Right. Biological hardware.

03:55.840 --> 04:00.820
Exactly. They were shaped by evolutionary pressures to keep social primates cooperating

04:00.820 --> 04:05.000
so they wouldn't die out on the savanna. The text rightly points out that humanism,

04:05.360 --> 04:09.000
this idea that human beings are the center of the universe and inherently special,

04:09.380 --> 04:15.040
has no more cosmic validity than what the author playfully calls Earthism or alienism.

04:15.340 --> 04:17.720
Yeah. We aren't the main characters of the universe.

04:18.040 --> 04:21.980
Exactly. We are just one species on one rock.

04:21.980 --> 04:29.120
But here is my primary objection to the essay. After doing all that heavy lifting to prove that

04:29.120 --> 04:34.660
human attachments are arbitrary, the author claims we should return to these attachments

04:34.660 --> 04:36.280
simply because, quote,

04:36.280 --> 04:38.100
I am human. Right.

04:38.100 --> 04:45.400
To me, that abandons the rigor of the first engine entirely too early. I suggest that what the author

04:45.400 --> 04:51.300
labels as wisdom or an existential choice is actually just a philosophical cover story.

04:51.700 --> 04:57.260
It's a rationalization for succumbing to the exact same evolutionary poles that were just debunked.

04:57.480 --> 04:58.560
Ah, so you're saying...

04:58.560 --> 05:03.160
You realize the house is an illusion, you knock it down, but you get scared of the dark,

05:03.160 --> 05:06.000
so you build the illusion again and just call it enlightenment.

05:06.500 --> 05:10.820
I see why you think that, but let me give you a different perspective on how reason and wisdom

05:10.820 --> 05:16.640
actually interact here. I think you are demanding that the return be justified by the exact same

05:16.640 --> 05:18.160
tool that caused the dissolution.

05:18.580 --> 05:19.300
Well, shouldn't it be?

05:19.600 --> 05:25.380
No. The author explicitly warns against this. Rational thinking clears the field of inherited

05:25.380 --> 05:30.600
illusions. It rips up the weeds. But reason cannot tell you what to plant in an empty field.

05:30.600 --> 05:35.200
If the return were a logical deduction, it would just be another weed to pull.

05:35.640 --> 05:36.640
Okay, but...

05:36.640 --> 05:41.860
Instead, it's an act of creation of meaning. Wisdom is the hand that knows when to put the

05:41.860 --> 05:47.180
solvent away and decide to build something. To demand a strict logical proof for why we

05:47.180 --> 05:51.860
should love our families is to fundamentally misunderstand what the second engine is designed

05:51.860 --> 05:52.340
to do.

05:52.660 --> 05:58.380
I hear you, but I'm sorry. I just don't buy that. Let me tell you why. If romantic love,

05:58.380 --> 06:04.340
ambition, and empathy are explicitly defined in the text as biological imperatives, blind

06:04.340 --> 06:10.300
optimization processes running for millions of years, how does slapping the label of wisdom on them

06:10.300 --> 06:12.120
change their fundamental mechanics?

06:12.520 --> 06:14.080
Because the awareness changes.

06:14.820 --> 06:20.800
But does it? Yes, you clear the field. But then you happen to plant the exact same seeds that your DNA

06:20.800 --> 06:25.800
engineered you to plant. You come back to emotions, you go back to caring about your community,

06:25.800 --> 06:31.720
and you claim it's because, quote, I am human, that is the bare fact, the material I have.

06:31.840 --> 06:33.320
Right, the situated reality.

06:33.760 --> 06:38.860
But returning to the human marketplace just because the void is empty looks suspiciously like our

06:38.860 --> 06:43.840
biological default. You aren't making some transcendent choice. Your nervous system is

06:43.840 --> 06:47.600
just doing what it evolved to do after your intellect got tired of the cold.

06:47.600 --> 06:52.660
That's a really interesting point, though. I would frame it differently. I think the issue is you are

06:52.660 --> 06:58.520
looking at the external action—the planting of the seeds, going to work, kissing your spouse—and

06:58.520 --> 07:02.940
you are seeing the exact same outward behavior as the person who never questioned anything.

07:03.060 --> 07:04.720
Because it is the exact same behavior.

07:04.900 --> 07:10.660
Outwardly, yes. So you assume the internal mechanism must be identical to the biological default.

07:10.660 --> 07:16.760
But the phenomenological reality of the person acting has completely transformed. It's like

07:16.760 --> 07:19.620
Saren Kierkegaard's concept of the Knight of Faith.

07:19.740 --> 07:20.480
The double movement.

07:20.640 --> 07:25.920
Right. The first movement is infinite resignation. You surrender every finite attachment,

07:26.180 --> 07:31.700
you accept the void. But the second movement is a total return to the finite world. You love it

07:31.700 --> 07:34.440
fully, but you do it knowing it lacks cosmic meaning.

07:34.660 --> 07:34.980
Mm-hmm.

07:34.980 --> 07:39.600
The person who has made the double movement looks exactly like a normal person walking down

07:39.600 --> 07:45.280
the street. But their internal state is radically different. And the author anticipates your exact

07:45.280 --> 07:50.340
critique here—this accusation that we are just biological robots reverting to our programming

07:50.340 --> 07:54.080
by bringing in the Buddhist concept of anatta, or no-self.

07:54.080 --> 07:59.040
Wait, let me make sure I understand how you're applying this. Anatta is about the dissolution

07:59.040 --> 08:04.300
of the bounded ego, right? The idea that I am not a separate, permanent entity.

08:04.620 --> 08:10.820
Exactly. The text makes it clear that the return is not driven by a small, anxious ego retreating

08:10.820 --> 08:16.080
to the house because the void is too cold. Instead, when you dissolve the small self, it actually

08:16.080 --> 08:17.780
produces total agency.

08:18.160 --> 08:19.060
Total agency?

08:19.380 --> 08:25.240
Ink of the Bodhisattva in Mahayana Buddhism. A Bodhisattva is an enlightened being who chooses

08:25.240 --> 08:30.840
to delay their own ultimate release to stay in the world and help all sentient beings.

08:30.840 --> 08:37.000
Why do they do that? Not out of a biological urge to protect the tribe, but precisely because

08:37.000 --> 08:40.040
they realize the boundaries between beings are illusions.

08:40.040 --> 08:41.080
Okay.

08:41.080 --> 08:47.960
The wanting that arises is no longer the defensive, survival-driven wanting of a single organism—it

08:47.960 --> 08:53.960
becomes the wanting of the whole. The choice to care is truly free because it is unbounded.

08:53.960 --> 08:57.160
You are no longer acting out of constrained biological panic.

08:57.160 --> 09:02.280
But notice the contradiction hiding right there in that formulation. You say it becomes

09:02.280 --> 09:07.080
the wanting of the whole, and the text says it's what unbounded being does in a particular

09:07.080 --> 09:12.360
human form. I have to strongly question the consistency of this unbounded agency.

09:12.640 --> 09:12.900
Why?

09:13.100 --> 09:20.580
Because the text literally quotes the Diamond Sutra, saying all forms are illusion. Okay, if all

09:20.580 --> 09:26.740
forms are illusion, why does this supposedly infinite, unbounded agent specifically privilege

09:26.740 --> 09:28.100
the human form?

09:28.100 --> 09:32.580
Because the human form is the situated reality we find ourselves in. We don't have a choice

09:32.580 --> 09:33.140
about that.

09:33.140 --> 09:39.220
But earlier in the essay, the author successfully dismantles humanism. They proved there's no

09:39.220 --> 09:44.900
cosmic reason to prefer humans over aliens or humans over the raw flourishing of the earth,

09:44.900 --> 09:50.660
which, as the essay playfully notes via a reference to the Matrix, humanity might actually be harming,

09:50.660 --> 09:51.620
like a virus.

09:51.620 --> 09:54.180
Right. Agent Smith's monologue.

09:54.180 --> 10:00.900
Yeah. We might be the bad guys of the ecosystem. Yet in the end, after achieving this boundless,

10:00.900 --> 10:06.820
egoless state, the author just defaults to human connection. Why? Simply by virtue of being,

10:06.820 --> 10:09.060
quote, thrown into being human.

10:09.060 --> 10:16.420
Exactly. But if you are truly an unbounded agent, how do you avoid the trap of simply adopting the

10:16.420 --> 10:22.500
biases of the physical vessel you happen to be riding in? It seems to me the bodhisattva just

10:22.500 --> 10:27.700
heavily favors the species of the nervous system it's attached to. I'm not convinced by that line

10:27.700 --> 10:33.940
of reasoning, because it ignores the inescapable existential reality of our physical situatedness.

10:33.940 --> 10:39.380
Unbounded agency does not mean you suddenly possess the capacity to act as a cosmic ghost

10:39.380 --> 10:40.420
floating in the ether.

10:40.420 --> 10:41.540
Well, obviously not.

10:41.540 --> 10:45.060
You are still physically located. The author states explicitly,

10:45.060 --> 10:49.860
there is no view from nowhere available to me. The return to the human isn't a declaration that

10:49.860 --> 10:54.820
humanism is cosmically superior to alienism. It is simply the recognition that the human

10:54.820 --> 10:59.700
is the material you have in your hands right now. It is a localized expression of a universal stance.

10:59.700 --> 11:05.460
So you're saying I can't build a house out of alien materials because I don't have them. I only have

11:05.460 --> 11:06.500
human bricks.

11:06.500 --> 11:12.180
Exactly. And this actually moves us into how the author justifies this whole endeavor.

11:12.180 --> 11:16.580
Since we can't rely on universal cosmic laws to tell us what to do,

11:16.580 --> 11:21.300
the author grounds the entire journey in what they call a pragmatic criterion.

11:21.300 --> 11:24.100
Ah, yes, the pivot to pragmatism.

11:24.100 --> 11:31.140
It's a crucial pivot. The author doesn't claim that pure rationality or free will are cosmic goods

11:31.140 --> 11:38.980
hovering in the sky. Autonomy is valued strictly because, empirically, it maximizes desirable outcomes.

11:38.980 --> 11:39.940
Right.

11:39.940 --> 11:45.940
Specifically, it positions us to ensure our consequential actions are doing good for humanity,

11:45.940 --> 11:51.860
or the world, or the universe. And to fully grasp how this works, we have to unpack how the author

11:51.860 --> 11:55.940
contrasts western and eastern approaches to the limits of our understanding.

11:55.940 --> 12:02.020
Let's break that down because the text gets incredibly dense here. It brings in Wittgenstein,

12:02.020 --> 12:05.620
Husserl, Heidegger, and Buddhist practice all in the same breath.

12:05.620 --> 12:12.260
Right. So let's take Ludwig Wittgenstein. In his Tractatus, he essentially mapped the boundaries of

12:12.260 --> 12:15.940
language. He drew a map of the island of logic and said,

12:15.940 --> 12:22.820
Look, anything off this island we cannot speak about. We must pass over the unspeakable in silence.

12:23.620 --> 12:26.420
He cartographed the edge, but he didn't cross it.

12:26.420 --> 12:30.020
He hit the limit of what reason could do, and just stopped.

12:30.020 --> 12:36.420
Exactly. But then the author points to the Buddha and western phenomenologists like Edmund Husserl and

12:36.420 --> 12:42.100
Martin Heidegger, who took a different approach. Husserl has this concept called the epique.

12:42.100 --> 12:47.700
Right. The bracketing. Yes. It basically means hitting the pause button on all your assumptions

12:47.700 --> 12:53.300
about whether the outside world is real. So you can just examine the pure, raw data of your experience.

12:53.860 --> 12:58.500
You bracket the world. And Heidegger talks about galassenheit, or releasement,

12:58.900 --> 13:03.700
stopping the urge to force the world to be useful to you, and just letting it reveal itself.

13:03.700 --> 13:08.900
So instead of trying to logically map the silence, they are trying to experience it.

13:08.900 --> 13:15.780
Yes. They use practice to orient their whole being toward that silence. The Chinese concept used in

13:15.780 --> 13:21.220
the text is guan, which means a kind of direct contemplative seeing. The return to the human

13:21.220 --> 13:26.420
isn't a logical proof that you can write on a chalkboard. It is a practice. It's direct seeing.

13:26.420 --> 13:30.820
It is a pragmatic orientation that yields the best outcomes for the reality we are thrown into.

13:30.820 --> 13:36.580
That's a compelling argument, and I appreciate how you've untangled those concepts. But have you

13:36.580 --> 13:41.460
considered the massive circularity sitting right at the heart of that pragmatic criterion?

13:42.180 --> 13:43.460
Circularity in what sense?

13:43.460 --> 13:48.420
Let's look at the sequence of events. The author claims that rationality is a tool,

13:48.420 --> 13:53.700
a solvent, used to clear away inherited conventions so we can advance outcomes we care about.

13:53.700 --> 14:00.580
And the text specifically defines those desired outcomes as doing good for humanity or the universe.

14:01.540 --> 14:03.140
Yes. That's the pragmatic goal.

14:03.140 --> 14:08.740
But wait a minute. If the goal of doing good for humanity was established before the dissolution

14:08.740 --> 14:13.060
phase began, then the ultimate value was smuggled in from the very start.

14:13.060 --> 14:15.540
Oh, I see what you're saying.

14:15.540 --> 14:23.940
The solvent of reason was never actually applied to the foundational premise of wanting to do good.

14:23.940 --> 14:30.340
Think about it. The author sets out to ruthlessly sledgehammer everything. Romantic love,

14:30.340 --> 14:38.020
ambition, patriotism. But miraculously, the desire to do good is exempted from the demolition.

14:38.580 --> 14:45.220
Reason isn't acting as an unbiased solvent here. It's being used as a highly targeted weapon

14:45.220 --> 14:50.580
to clear out everything except the author's pre-existing, biologically inherited desire

14:50.580 --> 14:56.260
for human flourishing. So you're suggesting the pragmatic criterion is a safe room the author

14:56.260 --> 15:02.340
built before demolishing the rest of the house? That is exactly what it is. If the void is truly

15:02.340 --> 15:08.340
real, if every standpoint dissolves under sufficient logical pressure, then the pragmatic criterion

15:08.340 --> 15:13.220
has to dissolve too. Why care about desirable outcomes at all? Why care about the universe?

15:13.220 --> 15:19.060
Well, the text attempts to bridge this gap by saying, well, given that I find myself caring

15:19.060 --> 15:25.780
about them, reason helps advance them. But that finding myself caring is exactly the biological and

15:25.780 --> 15:31.540
cultural conditioning the essay supposedly just dismantled. You can't claim total infinite

15:31.540 --> 15:35.300
resignation if you kept a pragmatic safety net in your back pocket the whole time.

15:35.300 --> 15:40.340
I hear the friction you're pointing out. I do. But I think that critique assumes the author is

15:40.340 --> 15:45.860
claiming to have reached a state of absolute objective metaphysical purity. But the text

15:45.860 --> 15:48.180
explicitly disavows that. It does?

15:48.180 --> 15:54.180
Yes. The author accepts that human beings cannot fully escape the concepts and biology stamped onto

15:54.180 --> 16:00.180
them. In fact, the essay flat out admits, quote, strict autonomy is a fiction.

16:00.180 --> 16:02.180
Then why go through the exercise at all?

16:02.180 --> 16:07.780
Because there is a world of difference between blind compliance and conscious participation.

16:08.340 --> 16:14.740
The pragmatic criterion isn't a smuggled cosmic truth. It is a confessed starting point. The

16:14.740 --> 16:19.460
philosophical journey isn't invalidated just because it starts from a human preference.

16:20.020 --> 16:25.140
The sheer power of the essay is in what happens after you recognize that your preference for human

16:25.140 --> 16:31.060
flourishing has no cosmic backing. The realization that the universe just doesn't care if we do good.

16:31.060 --> 16:37.380
Right. The shift is moving from saying, this is objectively honorable and the universe commands it,

16:37.380 --> 16:42.900
to saying, I know this has absolutely no cosmic foundation, but I choose to honor it anyway.

16:43.380 --> 16:49.220
There is a quote from the Diamond Sutra used in the text. Let the mind arise without dwelling anywhere.

16:49.220 --> 16:49.940
Okay.

16:49.940 --> 16:57.700
Not dwelling nowhere. Not dwelling somewhere fixed. It arises freely, attached to nothing, present everywhere.

16:57.700 --> 17:04.020
The circularity you see, starting with human care, going into the void, and returning to human

17:04.020 --> 17:10.180
care, isn't a closed loop. It's a spiral. You arrive at the same geographical location,

17:10.180 --> 17:12.980
but you are at a completely different elevation of awareness.

17:12.980 --> 17:19.860
A spiral or a closed loop, that is the multi-million dollar question. Because if I am choosing to honor

17:19.860 --> 17:26.020
something while knowing it has no cosmic foundation, and the thing I choose to honor perfectly mirrors

17:26.020 --> 17:31.940
the exact evolutionary imperatives of a social primate, reproduction, cooperation, emotional

17:31.940 --> 17:38.180
attachment, then the distinction between freely choosing without dwelling and just acting out my DNA

17:38.180 --> 17:40.260
sequence becomes practically invisible.

17:40.260 --> 17:42.420
You don't think the internal awareness matters?

17:42.420 --> 17:48.580
I think it matters to the person feeling it, certainly. I mean, I respect the profound elegance

17:48.580 --> 17:54.580
of the philosophical framing. The integration of Kierkegaard's Night of Faith with Mahayana Buddhism's

17:54.580 --> 18:01.060
Bodhisattva is beautifully articulated, and it gives us a really rich vocabulary for our existential dread.

18:01.060 --> 18:02.580
It really does.

18:02.580 --> 18:09.380
But I maintain that the boundary between reason and wisdom in this text is incredibly fragile. It leaves

18:09.380 --> 18:14.900
the door wide open to the possibility that this freely chosen return to the marketplace, going

18:14.900 --> 18:20.820
back to the office, participating in society. It might merely be our deep evolutionary programming

18:20.820 --> 18:25.940
reasserting its dominance the very moment our intellect stops actively suppressing it.

18:25.940 --> 18:32.580
And I maintain that reducing the return to mere biological programming completely flattens the

18:32.580 --> 18:37.540
phenomenological depth of the human experience. If you're listening to our discussion today,

18:37.540 --> 18:43.300
and you've ever felt that crushing weight of realizing your daily routines are arbitrary,

18:43.300 --> 18:47.700
this essay offers a genuinely mature philosophical path forward.

18:47.700 --> 18:49.700
A path through the void.

18:49.700 --> 18:57.460
Yes. Rationality ruthlessly dismantles our cosmic illusions. Yes. But instead of leaving us paralyzed in

18:57.460 --> 19:04.740
nihilism, it allows practical wisdom and unbounded agency to step in. It allows us to freely construct

19:04.740 --> 19:10.500
meaningful human connections, to choose the finite, to love your family or pursue a goal,

19:10.500 --> 19:16.180
while fully comprehending its ultimate insignificance. I think that is the ultimate expression of freedom.

19:16.180 --> 19:21.940
Which brings us to a fascinating point of convergence in our reading. Whether we conclude the return

19:21.940 --> 19:29.700
is an act of transcendent wisdom, as you argue, or a deeply disguised biological default, as I suspect,

19:30.260 --> 19:37.620
both sides have to agree with the essay's most powerful, unshakable premise. The universe offers zero

19:37.620 --> 19:42.900
inherent warrant for our actions. There's no cosmic honor, no universal high score.

19:42.900 --> 19:50.500
Nothing is cheering us on from the stars. Precisely. And that forces a massive shift. Moving from asking,

19:50.500 --> 19:57.860
is anything out there honorable, to do I choose to honor anything in here? That correctly places the

19:57.860 --> 20:03.300
inescapable burden of meaning squarely on the shoulders of the individual. You can't outsource your

20:03.300 --> 20:10.500
purpose to biology or society or the cosmos. You have to own it. Absolutely. And the way the text

20:10.500 --> 20:16.660
derives there is remarkable. It doesn't just stick to one tradition. It seamlessly weaves western

20:16.660 --> 20:22.420
phenomenological traditions, hustle suspension of judgment, Heidegger's releasement, with eastern

20:22.420 --> 20:28.820
contemplative practice, like the Diamond Sutra and the concept of no self. It uses these intersecting

20:28.820 --> 20:34.500
frameworks to address the very limits of what language and logic can actually do for us. Logic can only draw

20:34.500 --> 20:40.660
the map, but practice how you actually live your life day to day is how you enter the territory.

20:40.660 --> 20:46.740
It certainly demands that we examine our own values. It asks us to look closely at our lives and figure

20:46.740 --> 20:52.340
out whether we are holding on to our attachments as inherited obligations, or if we are holding them

20:52.340 --> 20:58.340
as conscious groundless choices. Which brings us right back to our empty dirt lot. You've taken the

20:58.340 --> 21:04.420
sledgehammer to your reality. You've cleared the foundation and now you have a brick in your hand.

21:04.420 --> 21:10.660
Are you laying it down to build a house because it is the only motion your muscles remember how to make?

21:10.660 --> 21:17.700
Or are you laying it down because, having stared into the vast and terrifying emptiness of the sky,

21:17.700 --> 21:34.020
you have freely chosen to build a home. We will leave that for you to decide.

